---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const ann = [
  ["Campolattaro",224],["Forchia",173],["Moiano",126],["San Giorgio la Molara",81],
  ["Arpaia",51],["Montesarchio",40],["Sant'Agata de' Goti",26],["Guardia Sanframondi",14],
];
const kids = [
  ["Giacinta","18 Sep 1883"],["Agata","8 Oct 1884"],["Giacomo","22 Jun 1887"],["Agata","29 Jan 1889"],
  ["Lucia","2 Aug 1892"],["Alfonso","2 Aug 1892"],["Alfonso Carmine","17 Jul 1894"],["Giacomo","2 Feb 1901"],
];
const shared = [
  ["Crisci","Arienzo (Falco in-laws), Arpaia (Zampiello in-laws), Forchia (Annecchino in-laws)"],
  ["Carfora","Arienzo and Forchia"],
  ["Porrino","Arienzo and Forchia"],
  ["Maione","Arienzo and Arpaia"],
  ["De Lucia","Arienzo and Arpaia"],
  ["Cimmino","Arienzo and Arpaia"],
];
---
<Base title="The Valle Caudina">
  <h1>The Valle Caudina</h1>
  <p class="lede">Three villages, four kilometres apart, in two provinces — and one marriage pool.</p>

  <h2>Why the family left Arienzo</h2>
  <p>For two centuries every Falco generation was born, married and buried at Arienzo. Then, in 1913, a
  son is registered at <strong>Arpaia</strong>; by 1930 the family is at <strong>Forchia</strong>. The
  Italian records alone never explained why.</p>
  <p>The Annecchino records do. Six hundred and eighty-one Annecchino entries were harvested from the
  Benevento civil registers, and they sort like this:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Town</th><th>Records</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{ann.map(([t,n]) => (
      <tr><td>{t}{t === "Forchia" ? <> <span class="chip doc">the Falco's new home</span></> : null}</td>
        <td class="mono">{n}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><strong>Forchia is an Annecchino town.</strong> Carminantonio Falco of Arienzo married
  <strong>Filomena Annecchino</strong>, and the family followed the wife's parish. That is the answer:
  the move north was a marriage, not a migration.</p>

  <h2>The Forchia household</h2>
  <p>One family fits the record exactly — <strong>Carmine Annecchino and Maria Carfora</strong>:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Child</th><th>Born</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{kids.map(([n,d]) => <tr><td>{n}</td><td class="mono">{d}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The family record gives Filomena's father as <strong>Carmine Annecchino</strong> and her
  grandfather as <strong>Giacomo</strong>. Under the Neapolitan naming convention the first son takes
  the paternal grandfather's name — and this Carmine named a son Giacomo, twice. Filomena, born
  10 April 1897, would fall neatly between the children of 1894 and 1901.</p>
  <p><strong>Her own act of birth has not been found.</strong> FamilySearch caps deep paging at about
  1,100 results and treats <em>Annecchino</em>, <em>Annecchini</em> and <em>Anicchino</em> as one name,
  so the harvest is incomplete. This is <span class="chip">inferred</span>, not documented, and it is
  set out here so it can be checked rather than inherited.</p>

  <h2>One marriage pool, not three villages</h2>
  <p>Read the three in-law networks side by side and the same surnames keep appearing across the
  provincial border:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Surname</th><th>Appears among</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{shared.map(([n,w]) => <tr><td><strong>{n}</strong></td><td>{w}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Arienzo sits in Caserta; Arpaia and Forchia in Benevento. The boundary matters enormously to
  archivists and not at all to the people who lived there. When Carmine Falco of Forchia married
  Francesca Zampiello of Arpaia in 1955, and his brother Raffaele married her sister Caterina, they were
  marrying the next village along — as their families had done for two hundred years.</p>
  <p class="small">The shared surnames are a pattern, not a proven kinship. Falco, Crisci and Carfora
  were all invented independently in many places, and two Criscis four kilometres apart need not be
  cousins. What the pattern does establish is the shape of the world these families moved in.</p>
</Base>
